foreign  [Music]  my name is sadika Watson and um so  that's me  um I uh work as a data scientist at Eli  Lilly the pharmaceutical company  I'm on the development team for a shiny  application that is used at Lily so  essentially my job is to wear this hat  the app developer hat  and this isn't just a normal shiny app  this is a complex production grade app  we get a new version of the app released  every month with new features and Bug  fixes and a lot of people in my  organization visit this app every day so  it's really important that the app is  functioning properly all the time so  with that in mind I'd like to begin with  a story about something that happened to  me back in May 2021 so I was in my home  office working on a new feature for the  app  but of course I was multitasking because  I was also keeping an eye on the  activity in our team chat window so one  of the uh users was saying she was  having some issues with the app it  wasn't populating the drop down menu  with the values that she was expecting  so the values were in the app before and  then all of a sudden she didn't see them  anymore  so one of the other developers  identified the issue and determined that  we would have to quickly release a new  version of the app that resolves the  issue with the drop down menu and that's  when I panicked because I realized that  meant two things were about to happen  first it meant oh first it meant that I  would have to put on another hat  uh my code reviewer hat so as a code  reviewer I'm responsible for reviewing  GitHub pull requests I have to read the  code to make sure it adheres to our  coding standards  um and then I have to run the app in a  local environment to make sure that all  the old features work with the new  features  I have to check the radio buttons the  drop down menus the contents of the  tables I have to make sure that the  database API in in the model API  connections are still working as they  used to right as we added these new  features and so if it passes those tests  then we deploy the app to the test  server  but then that means I have to put on yet  another hat right this time I have to  put on my app tester hat so as a member  of the app testing team I was one of  several people that had to manually  deploy the deploy the the deploy I had  to manually test the deployed version of  the app so I would have to open the app  in a web browser and then run a lot of  the same tests as before check the radio  buttons check the drop down menus check  the you know API connections database  connections all those other things that  I did before in the code review  um and so now the only difference is  that this is on the deployed version of  the app as opposed to the pre-deployment  version  now testing is very important it does  allow us to catch bugs so that we can  fix them before the new version of the  app is is released to production however  as one of the people that had to you  know who was responsible for actually  running the test I could say that this  was a really extremely tedious painful  um experience for me it was very  stressful  um I also felt undervalued right because  I'm thinking okay I have a PhD in  statistics and yet my job now is to  click this radio button right and or  check this drop down menu so I felt like  this wasn't a good use of my time  another issue is that I and the other  app testers had other priorities so we  weren't full-time app testers we could  only run the tests when we had time to  squeeze testing in and that meant that  managing all of those testing those  schedules across the different testers  meant that it could take a long time for  us to get through those tests and  actually go to the next stage of our  software release  so because uh so that was that was uh  increasing the amount of time it took us  to deploy the app  because it was so time intensive we had  to sparingly apply these tests so we  couldn't afford to run them as new  features were coming in we had to or use  multiple scenarios we can only do them  really once you know if if we could do  that and then lastly we found that  different testers were running the tests  with different conditions slightly  different conditions so there was some  subjective assessment with whether or  not the tests passed or failed so one  tester could say it passed and another  person running the same test would say  that it failed so that was a problem  so because these releases were such a  heavy burden for me I started looking  for a way to make this process more  efficient for me and for our team I was  already familiar with selenium I knew it  was a great tool for automating  interactions with the web browser and I  figured this might help automatically  check whether the UI elements were still  working on the deployed version of the  app I was somewhat familiar with the  rvest package and that it was great for  web scraping so I figured this might  help to programmatically extract the  contents of the web page and I could run  some sanity checks against it to make  sure everything's rendering properly so  selenium and Arvest could work together  on the deployed version of the app  my colleague Eric nant uh recommended  that I use the shiny test package to  test the code that other other  developers were submitting in their pull  requests  um so he figured that that might help to  automate  um some of the code reviews  um and then of course later I found out  that there was a another version of the  Chinese test package called shiny test  two that somebody will speak about later  and then finally the shiny test  documentation was recommending that we  also use the test that package and so  now I had all these tools there was a  bit of a learning curve but I took about  three months of my time and eventually  over the next three months I was able to  use those tools to create an automated  testing pipeline  let's see how the software release  workflow changed after introducing these  tools so in the code review stage in  blue we see the manual pre-deployment  testing during code reviews was replaced  with the shiny Test 2 script that  automates these same tasks and now the  code reviewer had sits on the head of  the bot that runs the test script  and then in Orange in the post  deployment testing stage I'm running the  manual test  um the the manual regression test I'm  replacing those with the suite of of  selenium test groups and then I get to  remove that heavy app tester hat from my  head and place it on the bot who was  happy to run those test script at least  he doesn't complain about it if he does  not feel happy about it  so those two changes had a significant  impact on our software release workflow  our code reviewers and app testers had  less work to do they weren't feeling  stressed out with each new release we  didn't have to worry about testers  balancing their testing duties with  other high priority work so we ended up  shortening our time to deployment from  five days to five hours which was pretty  pretty huge for our testing team uh we  could apply our testing criteria as  often as needed so no matter how many  releases we had during the month or a  feature um feature a pull requests that  we merged during the month and then  because the test logic was contained in  the test script that meant we could  consistently apply those uh that test  logic from one test to uh one from one  test run to the next so that removed  that variation in test results that we  were  so I've talked about this before on the  previous slides but here's where I get  to formally introduce the automated  testing pipeline that I'm proposing it  contains four key principles first I  believe you should automate the tests  right you should actually have some sort  of programmatic representation of your  test logic  um and this could obviously be you know  we could easily full an hour or maybe a  day talking about this but lots of  different things like you know how do  you recover safely from errors how do  you appropriately log the test results  how do you have this repository of all  your test artifacts maybe your  screenshots or things that were  downloaded in the course of running the  app you know those are that's what I  mean by automating the test or all of  that next it's important to have one or  more development servers  um that are completely separate from  your production server and that allows  us to see how the updated app works  without breaking the production version  that end users are interacting with and  then the next two principles have to do  with when we would run the test the  first is that I recommend running the a  test running some pre-deployment tests  so that you can integrate these features  safely you know in a safe space on your  development server without crashing the  version of your app that the end users  are using and then finally  um I would recommend running the the  post deployment  um check on also on the development  server and this is where we can more  closely mimic what the end users are  going to experience when they log into  the app using the web browser so again  pre-deployment and post deployment  that's that's really important  one question that comes up is whether  automation makes sense for a particular  use case or a particular web app and to  answer this question I I usually  um uh follow up with a couple follow-up  questions the first would be is the  logic consistent  um the test logic is a consistent  um you know from one run to the next so  if you can say your your step one step  two step three if you can actually have  all that logic  um and not have to worry about the  changes from one to the next  um that's one consideration and then the  second question would be  um whether the test logic can be  completely scripted so that you can get  the result with having to use any sort  of subjective judgment or having some  sort of human involved making that  decision there are other considerations  obviously so there's do you have the  infrastructure to support these  automation tools like shiny Test 2 or  selenium  um do you have time to do this right  there's a little bit of a learning curve  and a little bit of time investment in  terms of writing these test scripts but  in general I would say if you answered  yes to both of those questions then I  would say your app is a prime candidate  for automation  um  so I prepared a working example that you  can play around with on your own it's a  simple app that allows you to create an  ice cream recipe using various  ingredients like peanuts and raisins and  chocolate syrup and for the adventurers  if you want to add some chicken nuggets  to your ice cream no judgment here but  we allow you to do that so the GitHub  repo the GitHub repo linked at the  bottom has all the code needed to run  the app it also has the shiny Test 2 and  the selenium code that will allow you to  just kind of play around with this in  your own environment so I definitely  encourage you to check it out  I created a couple screencasts that  demonstrate how this would work so first  I'll show you the pre-deployment testing  with shiny test 2.  now if you're running the shiny test  script in the rstudio IDE Applause this  first you'll see that there's a run test  button visible at the top of the script  and if you click that you'll see um that  there's some activity happening over  here in the build tab that shows the  progress of the test run  this test script automatically opens up  this local version of the app in a web  browser window so I didn't do that it  did it on its own in Google Chrome and  then you'll notice that it's selecting  various check boxes and entering text  and the text field and doing all kinds  of things and showing how the app is  changing  so all of those things happened  automatically and so when I go back to  the our studio IDE I see that the test  result folder now contains a few files  that were saved in the test run and if I  click on one of those I've got  um some screenshots that show you know  what would the app look like at various  points in the app there's also this Json  file that shows the values of various  elements in the UI as they were at the  time that the snapshot was created if  you stick around for the next talk in  this session you'll actually get a  chance to see the package author of you  know the shiny Test 2 uh developer give  a presentation about shiny test two so  um I didn't spend a whole lot of time  thinking about that for this  presentation but I highly recommend that  you check that out and now finally I'll  show you what the post deployment  version of the testing looked like with  selenium  so as before we click the run test  button at the top uh we will also see  the test run progress in the build tab  in the upper right corner now before we  would actually open up automatically  this web browser in Google Chrome but  this is a headless browser so we don't  actually have access to that browser  instead it's just going to do it under  the hood and silently execute those Test  instructions that I provided  um and so we'll just see the  we'll see the mouse that I can't anymore  for some reason  where are you mouth  I see it on your side but not my side  for some reason  [Music]  okay there we go okay I'll pause that  here  um so uh what you saw and unfortunately  I wasn't able to uh to narrate that  while it was going while I was looking  for my Mouse  um was that um it was uh showing you  um how it was actually uh  programmatically running the script and  then it captured some screenshots along  the way as it progressed through the  various test scripts and it also in the  log area it let you know what was going  on in the app so it at this point it was  selecting the flavor and another point  it was selecting the the recipe uh name  and and so on  um so this was all um very easy and  straightforward thanks to the tools that  you know some of the other developers  have created um so I thank him for that  so in closing I like to re-emphasize  that these tools really make automated  testing more accessible to the general  population of data scientists especially  people like me who don't have any formal  uh training in software engineering or  software testing right we're kind of  expected to know a lot and do a lot  about a lot of different areas but this  this makes that uh that a lot easier for  us to do  um and then another thing that is out of  scope for this talk but I get asked this  all the time when I give a presentation  about automated testing  um you know how does this fit into the  CI CD pipeline you know it definitely  does and there's lots of tools that you  can use to do that in particular for  example if you had GitHub actions you  could  um programmatically run so as a person  submits their pull request you could  programmatically run your your tests on  the the pre-deployment version of the  app merge the pull request deploy to the  next test server and then actually  programmatically run that next  um set of scripts right so you can do a  lot of these things man uh very very  easy  so I'd like to thank my colleagues at  Eli Lilly in the design Hub Data  Insights information and digital  Solutions and statistical Innovation  Center uh themes for their help and I'd  also like to thank Wardell PCAT at PCAT  Studios for his help with the graphic  design in my presentation and here's my  contact information feel free to reach  out if you'd like to discuss anything  and I thank you for your time  foreign  [Music]

