oming in we had to or use  multiple scenarios we can only do them  really once you know if if we could do  that and then lastly we found that  different testers were running the tests  with different conditions slightly  different conditions so there was some  subjective assessment with whether or  not the tests passed or failed so one  tester could say it passed and another  person running the same test would say  that it failed so that was a problem  so because these releases were such a  heavy burden for me I started looking  for a way to make this process more  efficient for me and for our team I was  already familiar with selenium I knew it  was a great tool for automating  interactions with the web browser and I  figured this might help automatically  check whether the UI elements were still  working on the deployed version of the  app I was somewhat familiar with the  rvest package and that it was great for  web scraping so I figured this might  help to programmatically extract the  contents of the web page and I could run  some sanity checks against it to make  sure everything's rendering properly so  selenium and Arvest could work together  on the deployed version of the app  my colleague Eric nant uh recommended  that I use the shiny test package to  test the code that other other  developers were submitting in their pull  requests  um so he figured that that might help to  automate  um some of the code reviews  um and then of course later I found out  that there was a another version of the  Chinese test package called shiny test  two that somebody will speak about later  and then finally the shiny test  documentation was recommending that we  also use the test that package and so  now I had all these tools there was a  bit of a learning curve but I took about  three months of my time and eventually  over the next three months I was able to  use those tools to create an automated  testing pipeline  let's see how the software release  workflow changed after introducing these  tools so in the code review stage in  blue we see the manual pre-deployment  testing during code reviews was replaced  with the shiny Test 2 script that  automates these same tasks and now the  code reviewer had sits on the head of  the bot that runs the test script  and then in Orange in the post  deployment testing stage I'm running the  manual test  um the the manual regression test I'm  replacing those with the suite of of  selenium test groups and then I get to  remove that heavy app tester hat from my  head and place it on the bot who was  happy to run those test script at least  he doesn't complain about it if he does  not feel happy about it  so those two changes had a significant  impact on our software release workflow  our code reviewers and app testers had  less work to do they weren't feeling  stressed out with each new release we  didn't have to worry about testers  balancing their testing duties with  other high priority work so we ended up  shortening our time to deployment from  five days to five hours which was pretty  pretty huge for our testing team uh we  could apply our testing criteria as  often as needed so no matter how many  releases we had during the month or a  feature um feature a pull requests that  we merged during the month and then  because the test logic was contained in  the test script that meant we could  consistently apply those uh that test  logic from one test to uh one from one  test run to the next so that removed  that variation in test results that we  were  so I've talked about this before on the  previous slides but here's where I get  to formally introduce the automated  testing pipeline that I'm proposing it  contains four key principles first I  believe you should automate the tests  right you should actually have some sort  of programmatic representation of your  test logic  um and this could obviously be you know  we could easily full an hour or maybe a  day talking about this but lots of  different things like you know how do  you recover safely from errors how do  you appropriately log the test results  how do you have this repository of all  your test artifacts maybe your  screenshots or things that were  downloaded in the course of running the  app you know those are that's what I  mean by automating the test or all of  that next it's important to have one or  more development servers  um that are completely separate from  your production server and that allows  us to see how the updated app works  without breaking the production version  that end users are interacting with and  then the next two principles have to do  with when we would run the test the  first is that I recommend running the a  test running some pre-deployment tests  so that you can integrate these features  safely you know in a safe space on your  development server without crashing the  version of your app that the end users  are using and then finally  um I would recommend running the the  post deployment  um check on also on the development  server and this is where we can more  closely mimic what the end users are  going to experience when they log into  the app using the web browser so again  pre-deployment and post deployment  that's that's really important  one question that comes up is whether  automation makes sense for a particular  use case or a particular web app and to  answer this question I I usually  um uh follow up with a couple follow-up  questions the first would be is the  logic consistent  um the test logic is a consistent  um you know from one run to the next so  if you can say your your step one step  two step three if you can actually have  all that logic  um and not have to worry about the  changes from one to the next  um that's one consideration and then the  second question would be  um whether the test logic can be  completely scripted so that you can get  the result with having to use any sort  of subjective judgment or having some  sort of human involve